Revision history for SICK

{{$NEXT}}
    - Initial release
    - SICK::Config: the device list (YAML) with the ca, defaults and devices
      sections; unknown keys are an error with their path, type-specific
      keys of a device end up in its options; read_secret reads a secret
      from a file and never puts its content into a message
    - SICK::Bundle: certificate, chain and key as PEM plus what openssl
      reads from the certificate - names (DNS and IP SANs, the CN without
      SANs), validity, SHA-256 fingerprint, subject and issuer DN,
      is_issued_by
    - SICK::Issuer with SICK::Issuer::StepCA (the step CLI against a JWK
      provisioner of step-ca) and SICK::Issuer::SelfSigned (plain openssl,
      for tests and for trying things out)
    - SICK::Target: base class and plugin loader for device types
      ("cisco-sb" loads SICK::Target::CiscoSB), default presented_cert over
      TLS; SICK::Target::Files writes PEM files and runs a reload command
    - SICK::Decision: renew or not - missing, foreign-ca, names, expiring
    - SICK::Runner: check, issue, deploy, check again for every device;
      a device that fails or does not answer does not stop the others, and
      a deployment only counts when the device presents the certificate
      that was issued
    - sick, the command line: check, run (--force, --dry-run, --verbose)
      and trust; exit code 1 when a device failed, 2 for a wrong command
      line or device list
    - SICK::Target::Acmesh: hands the certificate to a deploy hook of
      acme.sh ("type: acmesh"), secrets from files reach the hook through
      its environment only; verified with acme.sh 3.1.6 and its localcopy
      hook
    - SICK::Trust and "sick trust": the root certificate as PEM, DER and
      Apple configuration profile, and a page with the fingerprints and the
      installation steps for each platform
    - "name" in the ca section: what the CA is called on that page
    - X.509 handling goes through the openssl command line, started without
      a shell; runtime dependencies are core Perl plus YAML::PP
    - "intermediates" in the ca section: the intermediate certificate of
      the CA. step-ca signs through an intermediate, and a device that
      presents its certificate alone (a switch with one certificate per
      slot) was "foreign-ca" on every run without it. The root file holds
      the root alone - step refuses a file with two certificates, and so
      does SICK now, saying where the intermediate belongs
    - SICK::Issuer::StepCA verified against step 0.30.2 and step-ca 0.30.2:
      the command lines were right; what step reports is now cleaned of its
      progress lines, colours and debug hint before it reaches the user
    - "foreign-ca" names the issuer readably: fields of a name that hold
      nothing but blanks are left out, no stray backslash
    - docker/: a Compose setup of step-ca (made at the first start, with a
      JWK provisioner for SICK and an ACME provisioner), SICK on a schedule
      and a web server for the page of "sick trust"; t/e2e/run-lab.sh
      proves it against a real switch
    - The example device list no longer sets ROUTER_OS_PASSWORD, which the
      routeros hook of acme.sh does not read (it logs in with an SSH key);
      the env_files example is the API token of the proxmoxve hook
